*** CRITICAL FINDING (drives spec.md §5/§7 hardening) ***

The installer probes EVERY --server given, in the order given, and SILENTLY DROPS any
unreachable one from krb5.conf/sssd.conf/default.conf entirely — it does not just reorder
or deprioritize it. With ipa1 stopped, listing `--server=ipa1 --server=ipa2` (ipa1 first)
still produced a client whose krb5.conf/sssd.conf/default.conf mention ONLY ipa2:

  krb5.conf   [realms]: kdc/master_kdc/admin_server/kpasswd_server = ipa2 ONLY (no ipa1 lines at all)
  sssd.conf:  ipa_server = _srv_, ipa2.ipa.pilot.internal          (no ipa1)
  default.conf: server = ipa2.ipa.pilot.internal, xmlrpc_uri = https://ipa2.../ipa/xml

This means Pilot passing a reachable-first-reordered --server list to ipa-client-install is
NOT sufficient to satisfy spec.md's H5 acceptance criterion ("bootstrap selector 必須將 ipa2
排第一，但 desired server pool 仍保留 [ipa1, ipa2]") — the installer's own connectivity probe,
not argv order, decides which servers land in the resulting config, and it unconditionally
excludes anything unreachable at enroll time. If Pilot only ran ipa-client-install and stopped,
a client enrolled during a primary outage would NEVER get ipa1 back into its failover list even
after ipa1 recovers, unless something reconciles it back in afterward.

CONCLUSION: the Phase 4 "server-failover reconciliation" step in spec.md §7's diagram — which
already runs right after "ipa-client-install fresh enrollment" for every enrollment, not only
for pre-existing clients — is not just an idempotent safety net, it is LOAD-BEARING for the
primary-down-fresh-enroll case (H5). Phase 3/4 implementation must run it unconditionally after
every fresh enrollment and diff the resulting krb5.conf/sssd.conf against the full desired pool
(§3's freeipa_server_pool), not just after detecting an already-enrolled host.

Sanity check (kinit against the surviving ipa2, post-enrollment): PASS (`KINIT_OK`).
